  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Fog and low stratus will impact travel conditions through\n  Wednesday morning across the region. \n\n- The remnants of an atmospheric river will move into the region\n  on Thanksgiving causing some precipitation and potential travel\n  impacts, particularly over mountain passes.\n\nHigh pressure will bring a period of quieter, more stable weather\nto western Montana and north-central Idaho through midweek. Even \nwith this stability, passing clouds and winds aloft will create a \ncomplicated mix of low clouds and fog across lower elevations. In \nsome locations this fog and low clouds may be stubborn to mix out,\nwhile other locations may see improvements through the day. \nMeanwhile, milder conditions are anticipated in the higher \nterrain.\n\nAs high pressure shifts eastward, light precipitation may develop \nas early as late Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, bringing \nminor snow accumulations to northwest Montana and the Idaho \nPanhandle. By late Wednesday, the remnants of a weakening plume of\nsubtropical moisture will move into the region with increasing \nclouds, breezy conditions, and widespread precipitation. While \nconfidence is high that this system will arrive, uncertainty \nremains regarding exact snow levels, which could range from valley\nfloors up to 4,000 feet by early Thursday morning. Because this \nweakening system lacks the strong winds needed to easily clear out\ntrapped cold air, western Montana valleys should expect wet snow \non Thanksgiving. Higher-elevation routes, including Lookout, \nMarias, and Lolo Passes, have only a 15 to 25 percent chance of \nreceiving two or more inches of snow, keeping accumulations minor \nwith low to moderate travel impacts.\n\nAn active weather pattern will continue from Friday through the \nweekend, bringing several rounds of snow showers. Mountain areas \ncan expect a few inches of snow per day, while some valleys may \nsee localized accumulations of up to a couple of inches. Daytime \ntemperatures are forecast to be warm enough to melt most valley \nsnow each afternoon, keeping overall impacts minor. However, \nanyone traveling at night following periods of precipitation \nshould remain alert for patchy ice on roadways.",
